---
import SimulatorLayout from '../../layouts/SimulatorLayout.astro';
import OsmosisSim from '../../simulators/osmosis/OsmosisSim.astro';
import { osmosisQuiz } from '../../data/quizzes/osmosis';

const toc = [
  { id: 'what-is-osmosis', label: 'What is osmosis?' },
  { id: 'tonicity', label: 'Hypotonic, isotonic, hypertonic' },
  { id: 'animal-vs-plant', label: 'Animal vs. plant cells' },
  { id: 'controls', label: 'How the controls change the model' },
  { id: 'terms', label: 'Key terms' },
  { id: 'formulas', label: 'Formulas' },
  { id: 'applications', label: 'Real-world applications' },
  { id: 'misconceptions', label: 'Common misconceptions' },
  { id: 'model-limits', label: 'What the model simplifies' },
  { id: 'takeaways', label: 'Key takeaways' },
];

const references = [
  {
    text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 5.2: Passive Transport. OpenStax.',
    url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/5-2-passive-transport',
  },
  {
    text: 'Clark, M. A., Douglas, M., & Choi, J. (2018). Biology 2e, Section 41.1: Osmoregulation and Osmotic Balance. OpenStax.',
    url: 'https://openstax.org/books/biology-2e/pages/41-1-osmoregulation-and-osmotic-balance',
  },
  {
    text: 'Betts, J. G., et al. (2022). Anatomy and Physiology 2e, Section 3.1: The Cell Membrane. OpenStax.',
    url: 'https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/3-1-the-cell-membrane',
  },
  {
    text: 'Agre, P. (2003). Aquaporin water channels. Nobel Lecture in Chemistry. The Nobel Foundation.',
    url: 'https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2003/agre/lecture/',
  },
  { text: 'Hall, J. E., & Hall, M. E. (2021). Guyton and Hall Textbook of Medical Physiology (14th ed.), chapter on body fluid compartments. Elsevier.' },
  { text: 'Taiz, L., Zeiger, E., Møller, I. M., & Murphy, A. (2015). Plant Physiology and Development (6th ed.), chapter on water and plant cells. Sinauer Associates.' },
];
---

<SimulatorLayout
  branch="biology"
  topic="osmosis"
  description="Interactive osmosis simulator: change solute concentrations inside and outside an animal or plant cell and watch water move, cells swell, shrink, burst or plasmolyze. Includes explanation and quiz."
  toc={toc}
  quiz={osmosisQuiz}
  references={references}
>
  <Fragment slot="intro">
    <p>
      Every living cell is surrounded by water and full of dissolved substances. <strong>Osmosis</strong> is the
      movement of water across a cell membrane from where there is less dissolved solute to where there is more. Use
      the simulator to place an animal or plant cell in different solutions and see how water movement makes it swell,
      shrink, burst or wilt.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <OsmosisSim slot="sim" />

  <Fragment slot="instructions">
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Choose a cell type</strong>: a red blood cell (an animal cell) or a plant cell with a cell wall.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Set the outside solution</strong> with the slider, or pick a preset such as distilled water or seawater.
        The change applies immediately, as if you moved the cell into a new beaker.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Watch the water molecules</strong> (small blue dots). They cross the membrane both ways; molecules that
        have just crossed glow. The arrows show the <em>net</em> direction of flow.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Read the measurements</strong> under the simulator: cell state, tonicity, net flow, crossings per second,
        volume and concentrations. Plant cells also show turgor pressure and water potential.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Compare experiments</strong>: press <em>Save run &amp; start new</em>. Your run is kept on the graph and a
        fresh cell starts — change one setting and watch the new curve. Up to three saved runs are shown.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Click or tap any part</strong> of the cell or solution to learn what it is.</li>
      <li>
        Use <strong>Pause/Start</strong> to freeze the model and <strong>Reset</strong> to restore the default cell and
        conditions. All controls work with a keyboard (Tab, arrow keys, Space).
      </li>
    </ol>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="what-is-osmosis">What is osmosis?</h2>
  <p>
    Particles in a liquid are always moving. When a substance is more concentrated in one place than another, random
    motion spreads it out until it is evenly mixed. This is <strong>diffusion</strong>. Osmosis is a special case of
    diffusion: the diffusion of <em>water</em> across a <strong>selectively permeable membrane</strong> — a barrier that
    lets water through but blocks many dissolved particles (solutes) such as salt ions, sugars and proteins.
  </p>
  <p>
    Solute particles attract water molecules and reduce how freely water can move. So a solution with
    <em>more</em> solute effectively has <em>less</em> free water. When two solutions are separated by a membrane that
    solutes cannot cross, water moves toward the side with the higher solute concentration. It keeps moving until the
    concentrations are equal or until something, such as a cell wall, pushes back.
  </p>
  <p>
    Water crosses cell membranes in two ways: slowly through the lipid bilayer itself and much faster through protein
    channels called <strong>aquaporins</strong>. Their discovery earned Peter Agre a share of the 2003 Nobel Prize in
    Chemistry. Cells that move a lot of water, such as red blood cells and kidney cells, have many aquaporins.
  </p>

  <h2 id="tonicity">Hypotonic, isotonic and hypertonic solutions</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Tonicity</strong> describes how a solution affects a cell’s volume. It is always a comparison between the
    outside solution and the cell’s contents.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Outside solution</th><th>Compared with the cell</th><th>Net water flow</th><th>Animal cell</th><th>Plant cell</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Hypotonic</strong></td><td>Lower solute concentration</td><td>Into the cell</td><td>Swells, may burst (lysis)</td><td>Turgid (firm) — the normal healthy state</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Isotonic</strong></td><td>Equal solute concentration</td><td>None (equal both ways)</td><td>Normal shape</td><td>Flaccid (limp)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Hypertonic</strong></td><td>Higher solute concentration</td><td>Out of the cell</td><td>Shrinks, crenates</td><td>Plasmolyzed</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Human blood plasma has an osmolality of roughly 275–295 mOsm per kilogram of water. That is why intravenous
    “normal saline” is 0.9% sodium chloride: it is close to isotonic with blood. In the simulator, about 300 mOsm/L is
    isotonic to the default cell.
  </p>

  <h2 id="animal-vs-plant">Animal cells vs. plant cells</h2>
  <h3>Animal cells</h3>
  <p>
    Animal cells have only a flexible membrane. In a hypotonic solution, water keeps entering and the cell swells. A red
    blood cell first loses its dimpled, biconcave shape and becomes a sphere; if it gains still more water, the membrane
    ruptures. The bursting of red blood cells is called <strong>hemolysis</strong>. In a hypertonic solution, red blood
    cells lose water and shrink into a spiky shape described as <strong>crenated</strong>.
  </p>
  <h3>Plant cells</h3>
  <p>
    Plant cells are surrounded by a rigid <strong>cell wall</strong> made mostly of cellulose. The wall lets water and
    solutes pass freely, but it resists stretching. In a hypotonic solution, the cell’s contents (the
    <strong>protoplast</strong>, including the large central vacuole) swell and press against the wall. The wall
    pushes back with <strong>turgor pressure</strong>, which stops further net water entry and keeps the cell firm. This
    is what keeps leaves and young stems upright.
  </p>
  <p>
    In an isotonic solution there is no turgor and the cell is <strong>flaccid</strong>; a plant made of flaccid cells
    wilts. In a hypertonic solution the protoplast loses water and shrinks away from the wall. This is
    <strong>plasmolysis</strong>, and it is usually damaging or fatal to the cell.
  </p>

  <h2 id="controls">How the controls change the model</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Outside solution</strong> sets the solute concentration around the cell. Lowering it makes the solution
      hypotonic; raising it makes it hypertonic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Inside the cell (starting)</strong> sets how much solute the cell contains. Because solutes cannot leave,
      this amount stays fixed. As water enters or leaves, the same solute is diluted or concentrated — watch the
      “Inside now” readout change while the cell’s solute particles spread out or crowd together.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Membrane water permeability</strong> changes how easily water crosses. It changes the <em>speed</em> of
      osmosis but not where it ends up.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The model calculates the rate of volume change from the difference between inside and outside concentrations (minus
    turgor pressure for plant cells). As water moves, that difference shrinks, so the cell changes size quickly at first
    and then levels off — the curved shape you see on the graph.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terms">Key terms</h2>
  <dl>
    <dt>Solute and solvent</dt>
    <dd>The solute is the dissolved substance (such as salt); the solvent is the liquid it dissolves in (here, water).</dd>
    <dt>Selectively permeable membrane</dt>
    <dd>A membrane that allows some substances, such as water, to pass while blocking others.</dd>
    <dt>Osmolarity</dt>
    <dd>The total concentration of dissolved particles, in osmoles (or milliosmoles, mOsm) per litre of solution.</dd>
    <dt>Tonicity</dt>
    <dd>The effect a solution has on a cell’s volume: hypotonic, isotonic or hypertonic.</dd>
    <dt>Aquaporin</dt>
    <dd>A membrane channel protein that lets water molecules pass rapidly in single file.</dd>
    <dt>Turgor pressure</dt>
    <dd>The pressure of a plant cell’s contents against its cell wall.</dd>
    <dt>Hemolysis, crenation and plasmolysis</dt>
    <dd>Bursting of red blood cells; shrinking of red blood cells; and the protoplast pulling away from a plant cell wall.</dd>
    <dt>Water potential (Ψ)</dt>
    <dd>A measure, used mainly in plant biology, of water’s tendency to move. Water flows from higher (less negative) to lower (more negative) water potential.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="formulas">Formulas</h2>
  <p>Osmotic pressure of a dilute solution (the van ’t Hoff equation):</p>
  <code class="formula">Π = i · M · R · T</code>
  <p>
    where <em>i</em> is the number of particles each formula unit releases (about 2 for NaCl), <em>M</em> is molarity,
    <em>R</em> is the gas constant and <em>T</em> is absolute temperature. At 25 °C, each 1 mOsm/L of solute corresponds
    to about 2.48 kPa of osmotic pressure.
  </p>
  <p>Water potential of a plant cell, the sum of its solute and pressure components:</p>
  <code class="formula">Ψ = Ψs + Ψp      with  Ψs = −i · C · R · T</code>
  <p>
    Adding solute makes Ψs more negative; turgor pressure makes Ψp positive. Net water movement stops when Ψ inside
    equals Ψ outside — check the “Ψ inside / outside” readout for a plant cell in distilled water.
  </p>
  <p>The simulator’s equilibrium volume follows the Boyle–van ’t Hoff relationship:</p>
  <code class="formula">V / V₀ = b + (1 − b) · (C_in,0 / C_out)</code>
  <p>
    where <em>b</em> is the fraction of the cell’s volume that does not take part in osmosis (0.4 for the red blood cell
    in this model).
  </p>

  <h2 id="applications">Real-world applications</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Intravenous fluids.</strong> Fluids given into veins are formulated with their tonicity in mind, because
      the wrong concentration could shift water into or out of blood cells. (This is an educational example only — see
      our <a href="/disclaimer/">medical disclaimer</a>.)
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Food preservation.</strong> Salting meat and fish or packing fruit in sugar creates a hypertonic
      environment that draws water out of bacteria and fungi, preventing them from growing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Watering plants.</strong> Wilting is what happens when plant cells lose turgor. Too much fertilizer or
      salty soil can make soil water hypertonic to root cells, pulling water out of the roots (“fertilizer burn”).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Fish and other aquatic animals.</strong> Freshwater fish live in a hypotonic environment and constantly
      gain water, so they produce lots of dilute urine. Marine fish lose water to salty seawater, so they drink water and
      excrete excess salt.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Kidneys.</strong> The kidneys recover water from urine by osmosis, using aquaporins whose numbers are
      regulated by hormones.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Desalination.</strong> Reverse osmosis plants apply pressure greater than seawater’s osmotic pressure to
      push water through a membrane, leaving the salt behind.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="misconceptions">Common misconceptions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>“At equilibrium, water stops moving.”</strong> Water keeps crossing in both directions; it is the
      <em>net</em> flow that becomes zero. The crossings readout shows this directly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>“In osmosis, the salt moves.”</strong> In osmosis, only water moves across the membrane. The solute stays
      where it is.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>“Water moves toward where there is more water.”</strong> The opposite: net water movement is toward the
      side with more solute.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>“Hypertonic” describes the cell.</strong> Tonicity describes the solution relative to the cell. A
      solution is hypertonic <em>to</em> a cell.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>“Plant cells burst in pure water.”</strong> The cell wall prevents bursting. Turgid cells are healthy.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="model-limits">What the model simplifies</h2>
  <p>
    This is a teaching model, not a precise physiological one. The cell is drawn as a two-dimensional cross-section, and
    the number of particles is far smaller than in reality. Time is slowed down: a real red blood cell reaches osmotic
    equilibrium in a fraction of a second. The model assumes no solute can cross the membrane, whereas real cells
    actively pump ions and some solutes (such as urea) cross membranes easily, which changes a solution’s tonicity. The
    bursting threshold is approximate; in laboratory tests, red blood cells typically begin to lyse in salt solutions
    of roughly half normal strength.
  </p>

  <h2 id="takeaways">Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Osmosis is the net movement of water across a selectively permeable membrane toward higher solute concentration.</li>
    <li>Tonicity compares the outside solution with the cell: hypotonic solutions cause swelling, hypertonic solutions cause shrinking.</li>
    <li>Animal cells can burst in hypotonic solutions; plant cells become turgid because their wall resists expansion.</li>
    <li>Water always crosses in both directions — equilibrium means equal rates, not no movement.</li>
    <li>Membrane permeability controls how fast osmosis happens, while concentrations control how far it goes.</li>
  </ul>
</SimulatorLayout>
